            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "calibrate_pose captures a reference pose and, from then on, sends every pose as it stands relative to that reference.\n\nWHAT CALIBRATING DOES:\nClick 'calibrate input' and it takes the pose currently at 'pose input' and stores the opposite of each joint's rotation. Every pose that arrives after that has each joint combined with its stored opposite. So in the captured posture every joint reads as the identity, 1 0 0 0, and away from it each joint reads as the turn it has made since, in its parent bone's frame.\n\nThat is how to make a performer's own stance the zero: have them hold it, click, and the pose becomes a measure of departure from it.\n\nNOTHING COMES OUT UNTIL IT IS CALIBRATED:\nBefore the first calibration, 'pose out' sends nothing at all - not the pose unchanged. Click 'calibrate input' while a pose is arriving, or send it a stored calibration.\n\nKEEPING A CALIBRATION:\nThe capture is not saved with the patch. It goes out of 'calibration output' as soon as it is taken - the stored opposites, one row per joint, not the captured pose itself - and sending those rows into 'calibration input' later restores it.\n\nANY NUMBER OF JOINTS:\nIt works row by row, so a 20-joint pose, a 37-row Shadow pose or a 22-joint SMPL quaternion pose all work, as long as the poses that follow have the same number of rows as the one captured. Each row is a quaternion w x y z; it must be a numpy array.\n\nSYNTAX:\ncalibrate_pose\n\nEXAMPLE:\ncalibrate_pose\n\nINPUTS:\n\npose input:\nThe pose. Each one is sent on, corrected, once calibrated.\n\ncalibration input:\nA stored calibration, from 'calibration output'. Replaces the current one.\n\ncalibrate input:\nClick to capture the pose arriving now.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npose out:\nThe pose relative to the captured one.\n\ncalibration output:\nThe calibration, sent when it is captured.\n\nRELATED:\ntarget_pose also captures a pose, but scores later poses against it rather than correcting them.\npose_adjust applies corrections you set by hand.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 950,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "calibrate_pose captures a reference pose and, from then on, sends every pose as it stands relative to that reference.\n\nWHAT CALIBRATING DOES:\nClick 'calibrate input' and it takes the pose currently at 'pose input' and stores the opposite of each joint's rotation. Every pose that arrives after that has each joint combined with its stored opposite. So in the captured posture every joint reads as the identity, 1 0 0 0, and away from it each joint reads as the turn it has made since, in its parent bone's frame.\n\nThat is how to make a performer's own stance the zero: have them hold it, click, and the pose becomes a measure of departure from it.\n\nNOTHING COMES OUT UNTIL IT IS CALIBRATED:\nBefore the first calibration, 'pose out' sends nothing at all - not the pose unchanged. Click 'calibrate input' while a pose is arriving, or send it a stored calibration.\n\nKEEPING A CALIBRATION:\nThe capture is not saved with the patch. It goes out of 'calibration output' as soon as it is taken - the stored opposites, one row per joint, not the captured pose itself - and sending those rows into 'calibration input' later restores it.\n\nANY NUMBER OF JOINTS:\nIt works row by row, so a 20-joint pose, a 37-row Shadow pose or a 22-joint SMPL quaternion pose all work, as long as the poses that follow have the same number of rows as the one captured. Each row is a quaternion w x y z; it must be a numpy array.\n\nSYNTAX:\ncalibrate_pose\n\nEXAMPLE:\ncalibrate_pose\n\nINPUTS:\n\npose input:\nThe pose. Each one is sent on, corrected, once calibrated.\n\ncalibration input:\nA stored calibration, from 'calibration output'. Replaces the current one.\n\ncalibrate input:\nClick to capture the pose arriving now.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npose out:\nThe pose relative to the captured one.\n\ncalibration output:\nThe calibration, sent when it is captured.\n\nRELATED:\ntarget_pose also captures a pose, but scores later poses against it rather than correcting them.\npose_adjust applies corrections you set by hand."
        },
